ukmt-maclaurin-2016-m6.body:
Let $B$ be the point such that $AUBP$ is a parallelogram. Its diagonals bisect each other, so $M$, the midpoint of $PU$, is also the midpoint of $AB$, and $AM=\tfrac12AB$.

Compare triangles $ABP$ and $RSA$:
- $PA=AR$ (sides of square $APQR$);
- $BP=UA$ (opposite sides of the parallelogram) and $UA=SA$ (sides of square $ASTU$), so $BP=SA$;
- $BP\parallel UA$, so $\angle BPA+\angle PAU=180^\circ$. Around $A$: $\angle SAR+90^\circ+\angle PAU+90^\circ=360^\circ$ (angles $\angle RAP=\angle UAS=90^\circ$), so $\angle SAR+\angle PAU=180^\circ$. Hence $\angle BPA=\angle SAR$.

By SAS the triangles are congruent, so $AB=RS$. Therefore $AM=\tfrac12AB=\tfrac12RS$. ∎
